      ],
      "source": "https://www.trempealeaupioneer.com/articles/how-ettrick-became-a-town-and-a-village"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Early settlers in Ettrick followed the creeks, with French Creek being settled in 1859 and South Branch in 1861.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.trempealeaupioneer.com/articles/how-ettrick-became-a-town-and-a-village"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Ettrick Cooperative Creamery was once the community's largest industry, handling over twenty-three million pounds of milk in a single year.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.trempealeaupioneer.com/articles/how-ettrick-became-a-town-and-a-village"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Local farmers in Ettrick shifted toward dairying because butter and cream traveled better to markets than grain did.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.trempealeaupioneer.com/articles/how-ettrick-became-a-town-and-a-village"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A photograph from 1918 shows Ettrick's Main Street as an unpaved road lined with frame storefronts and horse-drawn vehicles.",
